WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.052 --> 00:00:08.010
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: I
think everyone in the room

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.200
will agree that R is a
very active community.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.380
Some of the best data
scientists, statisticians,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.090
mathematicians out there.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.730
It's also had over the
last two decades, a very

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.410
healthy and steady growth.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.670
But year after year
I hear, can R scale.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.517
Can it deal with
very large data.

00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:26.100
So that's what we're
here today to do.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.320
To talk to you about
how you can scale your R

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.420
workloads on Google Cloud.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.480
Our agenda for today.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.370
We'll be talking
about the evolving R

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.420
ecosystem on Google Cloud.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.950
Three reference workflows
for training and serving

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.240
machine learning models.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.200
One, TensorFlow, two, Spark,
and any R library that

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.790
might be a favorite of yours.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.780
Finally, we'll close with a
Q&amp;A with an online Dory app?

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.400
What is a Dory?

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.920
Well, it's a Q&amp;A messaging app
that we use internally for Q&amp;A

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.190
at scale to make sure
everyone has an opportunity

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.450
to ask questions.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.660
If you go to our Google Cloud
Next app, find our session,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.740
you'll see a Dory Q&amp;A link.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.627
This will allow you
to compose a question

00:01:04.627 --> 00:01:06.960
as well as vote to move the
good questions up to the top

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.222
that you want to see answered.

00:01:08.222 --> 00:01:10.180
This will actually be
open for the entire week.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.888
So if we don't get to
all your questions,

00:01:11.888 --> 00:01:15.370
we'll make sure to answer them
directly in the app itself.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.140
So let's get started with the
R ecosystem on Google Cloud.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.030
I posed the question
around can R scale.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.840
One response might be,
does it really matter,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.550
is there enough demand for it.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:30.130
So two areas three look for our
distributed data processing,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.570
as well as deep learning.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.040
Two, workloads that require
lots and lots of computation.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.440
And if I do a quick
analysis on crane logs,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.190
the number of crane downloads,
from 2017 Q4 versus 2018 Q4,

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.480
TensorFlow and Keras libraries,
two deep learning frameworks,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.040
have doubled in usage.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.050
This really is a signal
that data scientists

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.480
are exploring deep
learning to solve new novel

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.730
ideas in new problem domains.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.400
Also, Sparklyr has
actually grown by 6x

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.680
during that same time
period, a great signal

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.630
that data scientists
are really pushing

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.670
the limits of one
machine and the memory

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.350
that that really
restricts you to,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.740
and looking for distributed
options on clusters.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.683
So Google Cloud should scale.

00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:11.350
I've been on Google
cloud for two years,

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.890
and I will say I felt
neglected as an R user.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.350
Of course, as a cloud
provider, developers

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.760
were our primary audience.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.710
We want to make sure
that developers can build

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.750
applications on the cloud.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.490
We provided client libraries
like Java, Node.js, Golang.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.990
Next, we needed to help data
engineers build these pipelines

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.840
and data stores, providing
a SQL interface, maybe

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.020
a Java API for big data
frameworks like Apache Beam.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.730
And then finally,
TensorFlow is still

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.447
central to our ML ecosystem.

00:02:37.447 --> 00:02:39.780
So naturally, Python was the
first class language choice

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.100
for ML engineers.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.830
But after two years
of feeling neglected,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.960
I really am happy
to talk about some

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.870
of the evolution of Google Cloud
to support the R community.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.720
Natively, we do have a lot
of notebook options now.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.830
We also give you options to run
Spark on a managed auto scaling

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.120
Spark service.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.300
Allow you to train TensorFlow
models or any custom R library

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:00.827
models.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:02.910
And really, a big thanks
to the overall community.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.160
There's a lot of packages
that have been contributed

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.070
from Mark Edmondson, Hadley
Wickham and the RStudio team

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.170
to really interface a
lot of the great big data

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.530
and ML services that
Google Cloud has to offer.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.800
So let's talk about how
this actually looks like.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.340
At the very top, we have lots
of different notebook solutions.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.690
Zeppelin, everyone's favorite,
RStudio, RID, RStudio,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.780
as well as a managed you Jupyter
notebook within our kernel.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.620
Then moving to the
very bottom left,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.830
we have ingestion
and storage options.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.720
The two main ones
we'll cover today

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.260
are Google Cloud Storage, or
object storage service, really,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.730
for unstructured data.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.810
Working with images, free
text files, audio samples.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.060
And then for structured
and semi-structured data,

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:47.420
we have BigQuery, our petabyte
scale fully server data

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.560
warehouse.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.710
As we move more into exploratory
work and data profiling,

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.110
what are our options to
explore and prepare that data?

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:57.170
We have a SQL syntax
on top of BigQuery

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.960
that allows you to crunch
through billions of rows.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.165
We also have Spark, again, the
managed auto scaling cluster

00:04:03.165 --> 00:04:04.790
that I talked about,
if you want to use

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.950
some of the more
statistical techniques

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:10.400
like PCA or dimension
reduction techniques that

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.420
are available through Spark's
native ML lib library.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.170
Now, this is where
things get interesting.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.420
Usually, I would move
on to the right side

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.727
and build a custom ML pipeline.

00:04:19.727 --> 00:04:22.310
But I do want to stop and think
about the bottom middle there,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.930
where before you
invest time in that,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.580
you should actually
look into if some

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.490
if our pre-trained APIs can
offer up some solutions that

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.690
can get you a good way there.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.550
We have a lot of
different pre-trained APIs

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.020
in the natural language space,
the image space, translation,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.460
audio transcriptions.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.250
And we actually have a
ML service built directly

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.310
into our data
warehouse, BigQuery.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.800
And on the right side, this
is where we have invested

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.620
and we believe we can add
value in building a robust ML

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.337
pipeline.

00:04:49.337 --> 00:04:51.420
And the three workflows
we're going to cover today

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.930
are TensorFlow and Keras
on a managed service

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.580
called Cloud Machine
Learning Engine.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.130
We'll have Sparks ML
lib library outside

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.148
of just the data processing
techniques on Dataproc.

00:05:01.148 --> 00:05:02.690
And then the number
one question I've

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.750
gone over the last year,
what about any R library?

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.560
I like to use my statistics
libraries, my caret, my R part.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.840
How can we use a container
based approach to actually

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.550
run those at scale as well.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.300
So before I jump into
the workflows, which

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.420
we'll primarily focus on, I
want to just give you also,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.730
two quick examples
of how to embed some

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.440
of these workflows upstream.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.260
So let's say I want to
query lots and lots of data.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.870
On the left side, I'm
loading bigrquery.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.640
This is created
by Hadley Wickham.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.220
And with a few lines of
code, nothing special,

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.980
your normal SQL syntax,
but this exact query

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.680
actually crunched
through 100 billion rows,

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.690
3 and 1/2 terabytes
in 45 seconds.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.690
And then it downloads it
as a data frame for you

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.780
to then run ggplot and
other various analytics,

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.130
and [INAUDIBLE] on top.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.350
So very powerful for
quick exploratory work.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:53.380
And really allows you to
push all that scale work

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.390
to our managed service,
and then bring what you

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:57.580
need into your R data frame.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.410
On the other side
of things, let's say

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:00.730
I work for customer service.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.850
And I want to analyze all the
customer logs for sentiment

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:06.093
and what our customer is
actually calling about.

00:06:06.093 --> 00:06:08.260
Usually, I would need to
go to probably go to school

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:09.873
and get a deep learning degree.

00:06:09.873 --> 00:06:12.040
I would then maybe have to
look for a word embedding

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.020
and understand the relationships
between the different words.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.240
Having gone to creating
a labeling data

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.280
set that is required
for machine learning.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.760
So before I invest in all
that or hire a team member who

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.770
can do that, here's
a few lines of code

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.620
where you can access our
Natural Language API.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.290
The Google language R
API, that was contributed

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.230
by Mark Edmonson, that allows
you with a few lines of code

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:35.320
to analyze the text
extract various entities

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.480
ranked by salience
or importance.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.100
To classify the text
into various categories,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.300
as well as then look
at various sentiments.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.070
Now this might not
be your end all be.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.520
All this might be an upstream
data enrichment exercise

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.007
where you then combine some
of these outputs with some

00:06:51.007 --> 00:06:53.590
of your structured data to build
a more traditional structured

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:54.860
data model.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.710
So now let's dive into
the actual workflows,

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.340
and the first one is TensorFlow.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.000
The R studio team
has been working

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.160
alongside our TensorFlow
team to really

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.980
build a great set
of R APIs interfaces

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:09.610
on top of the TensorFlow
Python modules.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.540
On the very left side, we have
our three core R packages.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:15.940
Starting from the bottom,
we have TensorFlow.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.040
This is the lowest
level API that

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.400
allows you to build the graph of
operations, gives you the most

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.680
flexibility and customization.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.470
Moving onto the
middle, TF estimators.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.110
This allows you to use out
of the box algorithms--

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.350
regression, classification,
some of your common neural net

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:33.130
architectures.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.620
As well as even moving into
spaces like support vector

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.520
machines.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.910
And, finally, we have Keras,
our top level high level

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.270
API that's really built for
usability and quick prototyping

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.842
and rapid experimentation.

00:07:44.842 --> 00:07:46.300
And then on the
right side, we have

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.970
a lot of complementary
supporting libraries.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:50.443
We have TF data sets.

00:07:50.443 --> 00:07:52.360
A lot of times, when
you're building a machine

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.810
learning model, the
training process

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:56.560
doesn't always have
to be your bottleneck.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.060
It might be how
quick you're feeding

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.280
all the data in the
appropriate file formats.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.550
So TF datasets allows you to
work with different file types

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.650
to look at data data
parallel pipelines.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:10.180
TF runs, tracking, visualizing,
monitoring those lost curves,

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.830
looking at evaluation metrics.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.310
For those of you who have
explored TensorFlow on Python,

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.260
this is very analogous
to the TensorBoard tool

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:20.020
directly in your R UI.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:21.595
Third is tf.

00:08:21.595 --> 00:08:24.868
While you'll use R to build and
train your TensorFlow models,

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:26.410
one of the main
powers of TensorFlow,

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.260
it is a language agnostic
system, a framework.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.060
Where any other
downstream application,

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.429
whether it be Java,
C Sharp, can all

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.179
consume all those predictions.

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:37.750
So TF deploy allows you to
export, and manage, and save

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.220
all those models moving forward.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:42.610
And then finally, our
core library, Cloud ML,

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.520
is an interface to Cloud Machine
Learning Engine, a managed

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.090
service that allows you to
provision a cluster of machines

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:52.330
with GPUs or CPUs, as
well as also hosting

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:53.950
the models as Cloud
Endpoints for you

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:57.250
to consume in terms of an
instance level or batch level

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:58.730
prediction.

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.110
So what does this look like in
a more holistic architecture?

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:05.080
We do have RStudio Server
Pro available with one click

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:08.110
deployment through
our GCP marketplace.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.450
The code can be version
controlled within a Cloud

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.597
Source repository.

00:09:11.597 --> 00:09:13.180
This is a private
git repo, but you're

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.760
welcome to use GitHub
a Bitbucket as well.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.340
Once you package
your TensorFlow up--

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.200
and then let's say we're working
on a deep learning model--

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.830
we would store all our images
in the bottom left there

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.050
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.000
Then we'll package
the TensorFlow code.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.010
And the main piece
here again is you

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:31.600
might be doing like
development work

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.020
on the environment your RStudio
notebook is based off of,

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.500
but you're going to actually
submit the ML training as a job

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:39.940
to Cloud Machine
Learning Engine.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.225
You'll be able to only
provision resources

00:09:42.225 --> 00:09:43.600
for a number of
minutes, a number

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.460
of hours with the
appropriate hardware

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.420
accelerators that you
need at that time.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.360
So it's a very job
focused mindset

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.100
from an always running
development environment.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.640
Next, we're going to save
and store our results as well

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.740
as our model binaries in
Google Cloud Storage bucket,

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.400
and a hierarchy of
your choice to really,

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:01.820
version them as needed.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:05.620
And, finally, ML Engine also has
a secondary module for serving.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.650
This is where a model
essentially automatically gets

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:12.070
containerized and hosted
as a [? client ?] end point

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.010
for you to be able to
access as a REST API,

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:17.440
for instance level,
one time predictions,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.450
maybe for a web or
mobile application.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:23.080
Or if you need batch predictions
for running through millions

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.940
or billions of rows
of a table, you

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.260
can run the batch
option as well.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.680
Also interestingly, ML engine
is a cloud based solution.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.360
You have to be connected to it.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.030
RStudio Connect actually
is a very similar offering

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.940
where you can maybe host that
on premise on a server as well,

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.470
and host some of
those models, where

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.300
you might use our burst
compute to train the models,

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:43.210
but then still want to
serve them on premise.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:44.980
And then finally,
the API call can

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.600
be consumed by various
downstream applications,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.510
whether you stay
in the R ecosystem

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:53.830
with R Shiny or other
mobile and web applications

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.670
Let's dive a little bit now into
the actual code for training

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:01.420
on Cloud ML Engine again just
loading the cloud ML library

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:04.210
cloudml_underscoretrain
is the main function

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.060
where you actually just point
towards your TensorFlow code

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.998
that's sitting in a R file.

00:11:08.998 --> 00:11:10.540
There's a lot of
optional parameters,

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:12.310
and this is really
where the power is.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.410
The master type
or the scale tier

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.590
allows you to access those
resources for only the time

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:19.015
that training is taken.

00:11:19.015 --> 00:11:22.390
A standard GPU gives you
access to a Nvidia K80.

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.340
We have a P100.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:25.258
That's also an option.

00:11:25.258 --> 00:11:27.550
But if you actually want to
even scale further and have

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.790
multiple GPUs on one
machine, we have up to eight

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:34.840
V100s on one virtual machine
with a complex, large model

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:35.360
there.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.450
So with quick plug
and play, you really

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.330
have access to various
choices to really optimize

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:41.813
both price as well
as performance

00:11:41.813 --> 00:11:43.480
when you're training
models and thinking

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:46.270
about this on a job mindset.

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.700
Next we have deploy and predict.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.550
We're going to load the
Cloud ML library once again.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.300
Once you've really
trained the model,

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.330
you're going to have a directory
where that model is saved.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:57.710
You can export that model.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:00.010
In this case, I'm
calling it "savemodel."

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.310
And then I'm going to deploy
and host it as an endpoint.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.920
In this case, I'll
call it "keras_mnist."

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.170
Now at that point, you have
a lot of different options

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.090
from downstream applications.

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.340
But if you wanted to run
batch predictions or sample

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.480
predictions directly
in the R environment,

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.620
you can use the cloudml_predict
options to cycle through

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:20.620
and see whether the images
are being predicted correctly,

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:23.110
whether audio samples are
being transcribed correctly,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.210
or any other traditional
structured data problems

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:27.270
that you may be solving.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:29.470
So the great thing is
JJ Allaire from RStudio

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.920
was actually at
Next two years ago

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:32.850
introducing a lot
of these libraries.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:35.370
So this has been around
for almost two years now.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.570
So this is a rich ecosystem
where RStudio has, actually,

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:41.275
great resources, tutorials,
galleries, samples for you

00:12:41.275 --> 00:12:44.030
to take a look at, various
use cases across industries--

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:46.670
everything from health care,
to traditional time series

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:50.073
forecasting, which gets a boost
with deep learning techniques,

00:12:50.073 --> 00:12:51.240
as well as natural language.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.830
So please check that out.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.832
And this is probably very
mature, currently, out there.

00:12:55.832 --> 00:12:58.040
So now I'll kick it off to
Chris to talk a little bit

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.513
about Spark with R on GCP.

00:13:00.513 --> 00:13:01.680
CHRISTOPHER CROSBIE: Thanks.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:02.780
So I am Chris.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.570
I'm a product manager here at
Google Cloud for our open data

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.910
and analytics team.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:10.970
And I'm here to talk
about how Spark,

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:12.800
combined with
Cloud Dataproc, can

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.710
help you scale your R analysis.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:18.200
Now if you're not
familiar, Cloud Dataproc,

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.700
that is our managed
Hadoop and Spark service.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.010
And many of you probably
hear the word "Hadoop"

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:26.810
and zone out instantly,
thinking, OK,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.060
that old legacy technology.

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:31.370
And that's valid in some ways.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:35.700
But Spark is still a very active
community for machine learning.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.610
In fact, in two
weeks, right here,

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.280
there's going to be a
Spark AI conference.

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:43.850
And the reason why, it's
because Apache Spark,

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:46.130
it's a computing
framework that lets

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:49.460
you scale across a
cluster of computers

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.500
in a generalizable way.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:54.650
And that's perfect for
building a rich ecosystem of ML

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:56.540
libraries.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:58.130
And the other great
thing about Spark

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:00.180
is it lets us write our R code.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.060
And that means
that Cloud Dataproc

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:06.297
can scale your existing R
analysis without requiring

00:14:06.297 --> 00:14:08.840
substantial code rewrites.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.060
Cloud Dataproc is
often the fastest way

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:16.420
to move your analysis and
ML, with R, into the cloud.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:18.690
So our goal with
Cloud Dataproc--

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:22.740
we want to let you take the
open source tools, algorithms,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.860
and programming languages,
like R, that you

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:30.280
used today, but make it easy to
apply them to cloud-scale data

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:31.470
sets.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.740
And then you can do that
without having to manage tool--

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.380
clusters and computers.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:41.970
Now there's three ways to
run R on Dataproc today.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:45.800
There's RStudio, which
is the standard web

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:50.360
IDE that can be auto-installed
on the Dataproc master node.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.210
And then there's two different
packages for running R

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.660
on Spark, sparklyr and SparkR.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:58.485
And they have a lot
of similarities,

00:14:58.485 --> 00:15:00.110
but there are some
differences that I'm

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:03.040
going to explain as well.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:06.490
So sparklyr, that's a package
for working with R on Spark

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.040
that was developed by RStudio.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:10.540
So you're going to see a lot
of great hooks and integrations

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:13.510
between RStudio and sparklyr.

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:17.915
SparkR, that's built
directly into Spark itself.

00:15:17.915 --> 00:15:20.290
And so you're going to find
great hooks there with things

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:26.590
like Cloud Dataproc's jobs
API, which-- and that jobs API,

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.870
that makes it really easy
to submit jobs through--

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.710
submit R jobs through--
things like the gcloud command

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:34.760
or an HTTP endpoint.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:39.950
So you can build automated
tooling around your R code.

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.340
But what I'd like to do now
is just take a quick peek

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:45.160
under the hood of both
SparkR and sparkly

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.590
to help you understand
how these tools actually

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.850
scale your R analysis.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:54.710
So this is a very high-level
SparkR architecture.

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:56.390
There's two pieces to Spark.

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:59.120
There's going to be the
Spark driver, and then

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.060
the Spark workers--

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.380
again, very high-level.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:05.900
But oftentimes,
this Spark driver,

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:07.580
depending on what
mode of Spark you're

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:10.370
in, that's often going to
run exactly where your R

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.140
Studio's running.

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:15.620
And you're going to spin up
what's called a SparkR context.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:18.440
You can think about that
essentially as a client

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.140
for calling into Spark.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:24.800
Now, under the hood, what
happens in that SparkR context

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:25.730
is there's a bridge.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:27.230
It's done over a
socket layer that's

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:30.440
going to translate your
R code into Java code.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.960
And then there's a
Java Spark context

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.180
that's actually going
to scale your work

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.243
across the various
Spark executors.

00:16:37.243 --> 00:16:38.660
You can think of
those, basically,

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:42.660
as nodes in the cluster
at a high level.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.550
Now, what's going to happen
from those Spark executors is

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.390
it's going to actually
make a call out

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:51.380
to a local version of R. And
so that's real R. That means

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:54.410
any package that you can
take with R and scale,

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:58.280
you can scale with Spark--

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:02.180
SparkR rather-- because this is
real R code that you're running

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:02.930
at the--

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.400
once it's broken up.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:10.020
Now alternative architecture
to that is sparklyr.

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.940
Now what's sparklyr
does instead is

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.569
they're going to serialize the
information into Apache Arrow.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:17.849
And that means, in
terms of ML algorith--

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:19.878
and then what they
do is they call out--

00:17:19.878 --> 00:17:21.420
they use that
information to call out

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.730
to the spark.ml package.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.190
So that means, in terms
of machine learning,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.680
you're going to be limited
to the functionality that

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.510
exists in the spark.ml package.

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:33.660
So there are some--
now despite that, there

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:36.540
are some pretty cool features
I found with sparklyr

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.670
that are missing from SparkR.

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:42.210
So for instance, they do have
a direct integration with CRAN.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.790
They have an XGBoost extension.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.770
And they also
integrate with broom.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.070
If you're not
familiar with broom,

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:52.010
it's a pretty neat ML package.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.112
It takes the output of a lot of
unstructured, messy data sets.

00:17:55.112 --> 00:17:57.570
Like, if you have a bunch of
t-tests and it comes back with

00:17:57.570 --> 00:18:00.192
a bunch of unstructured
information, it'll--

00:18:00.192 --> 00:18:02.400
broom will take it and turn
it into a nice, tidy data

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:06.180
frame that will let you look
at results of multiple models

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:07.150
at once pretty quickly.

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:09.365
It's pretty-- it's interesting.

00:18:09.365 --> 00:18:11.490
So of course, this is going
to beg the question I'm

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:12.698
sure everyone has right now--

00:18:12.698 --> 00:18:16.110
OK, what do you want me to
use, SparkR or sparklyr?

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:20.580
So SparkR, I find that folks
use it a little bit more

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:21.867
for automation.

00:18:21.867 --> 00:18:23.700
First of all, it's built
into Cloud Dataproc

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.533
and to Spark itself,
so you get a little bit

00:18:25.533 --> 00:18:27.060
better performance.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:30.510
But sparklyr, again, that's
coming from the Tidy RStudio

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:31.350
team.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:34.170
So that does tend to have a
little bit better integrations

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:35.780
with RStudio.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:39.210
SparkR that comes from AMPLab,
the same people who did Spark.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.790
So you're going
to find that they

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:42.630
are going to follow
things in a little bit

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.345
more Spark-first kind of way.

00:18:45.345 --> 00:18:47.220
In other words, it's
going to follow, mostly,

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.080
the Scala way of doing things.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.980
Whereas sparklyr, they think
a little bit more R-first.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.590
And then sometimes they'll
do an R integration

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:58.020
at the sacrifice of
something like performance.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.900
But both are active projects.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.240
And so when there are examples
like that, what you find

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.700
is they tend to get
resolved pretty quickly.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:07.380
So oftentimes it
just comes down to,

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.255
where you want to spend
your time, automating

00:19:09.255 --> 00:19:15.400
R code, SparkR, or is it working
with interactive RStudio-type

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:15.900
analysis?

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.148
And then you'll go to sparklyr.

00:19:18.148 --> 00:19:20.440
But if you're still a little
confused on the difference

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:21.290
and what to use.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.210
Don't worry, I probably am too.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:27.210
But luckily, I found it's not
really an issue to use both.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.890
So all you-- once you
load the sparkly--

00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:33.590
the sparklyr package, you can
then just call into SparkR.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:36.500
It does step on some of
the deep layer functions.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.130
But as long as you
mask it correctly,

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:39.770
you can go ahead and use both.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:43.700
And a code-- a set of code that
has both sparklyr and SparkR

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.610
can be submitted to
the Dataproc jobs API.

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:53.250
So what I'm going to do now is
I would like to quickly walk

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:57.630
through a example of how you
can use Cloud Dataproc to scale

00:19:57.630 --> 00:19:58.708
some R analysis.

00:19:58.708 --> 00:20:00.750
And what I'm going to show
you is a common model,

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.340
like the one that you see here.

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:04.170
We're going to run
through a workflow

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.960
where the data starts in
BigQuery, which is often

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.330
a common store for
semi-structured or structured

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:10.410
data sets.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:13.020
We're going to spin up an
auto-scaling Cloud Dataproc

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:15.420
cluster to actually do
our machine learning.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:17.670
And then we're going to save
the output of that model

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.590
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:22.290
And that way, later on,
when I want to rerun this,

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:24.540
I'm going to use a
Cloud Dataproc workflow

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:29.250
template, which is a set--
it's basically a graph of jobs

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:31.140
that you can send to Dataproc.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.710
It'll spin up a right-sized
cluster to run those jobs,

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:38.640
run those jobs, make sure
that the cluster is torn down,

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.740
and then save all the
output of what happened

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.770
and all the logs
into Stackdriver.

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:50.100
So Mikhail, do you want to take
over on your laptop, flip over?

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:54.590
OK, go ahead and kick us off.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:56.830
OK, so essentially, I'm
a product manager here

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:57.850
at Google Cloud.

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:00.840
So a lot of time I
spend essentially

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:04.583
building articles or blogs that
I put out onto the internet.

00:21:04.583 --> 00:21:06.250
And what I really
love-- what I'd really

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:08.320
love to have is a machine
learning model that

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:11.470
would read my blog and then
say, how successful is this

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:13.040
going to be on the internet?

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:14.582
And that might give
me an opportunity

00:21:14.582 --> 00:21:17.110
to go back and tweak some
things before I actually go

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:18.730
and try to publish it.

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:21.580
So, whenever I'm looking
for data sets like this,

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:24.800
I always start with
BigQuery's public data

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:28.480
sets program that has over 180
different publicly available

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:31.600
data sets that are stored for
free that I can take and use

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:33.545
to augment my analysis.

00:21:33.545 --> 00:21:35.170
And what I do-- and
what I'm doing here

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:40.390
is I found a great data
set for Hacker News.

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:42.880
And Hacker News data
set, it has, basically,

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.910
a score, which is how well that
article did on the internet.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:49.900
That score is a combination
of upvotes and relinks.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:52.580
And it also has the full
text of the article.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:55.120
So I can use that to train.

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.770
Now, what you're
seeing in this query

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:59.380
here is just I'm taking
scores greater than zero

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:01.900
and a length of text greater
than zero, only to get a nice,

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:02.943
clean data set.

00:22:02.943 --> 00:22:04.360
Because what I'm
trying to do here

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:08.710
is-- although BigQuery
is awesome at SQL,

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:10.480
I wanted to take
advantage of R and some

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.550
of the machine learning
capabilities that Cloud

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:13.880
Dataproc can offer me.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:16.695
So what I'm going to do is just
going to put this into a table.

00:22:16.695 --> 00:22:18.070
I could go directly
with a query,

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:19.903
but I'm just going to
make it easy on myself

00:22:19.903 --> 00:22:22.030
and take a thousand-row
sample, and just

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:25.425
put it into this Hacker
News sample data set.

00:22:25.425 --> 00:22:27.550
And you can see-- in the
bottom of the screen here,

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:28.600
you can see what
I'm getting back,

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.183
which is a score
that I'm going to use

00:22:30.183 --> 00:22:32.660
for a label, along
with the full text.

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:35.830
So once that job has actually
completed, what I'm going to do

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:38.530
is I'm going to jump back
over to Cloud Dataproc.

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:41.570
And I'm going to
click on my cluster.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:43.960
And I'm going to come
into a web interface.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:48.850
Using our component gateway,
we expose various web UIs that

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:50.170
are running on your cluster.

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:52.690
And then we make sure that
you can come into those using

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.380
your cloud IAM credentials.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:56.590
So you saw me just jump
into Zeppelin, which

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:58.180
is a Spark-based notebook.

00:22:58.180 --> 00:22:59.943
But it also can work with R.

00:22:59.943 --> 00:23:02.110
And so the first thing that
you're seeing me do here

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:04.398
is just-- that same
query I ran in BigQuery,

00:23:04.398 --> 00:23:06.190
I could have run that
directly in Zeppelin.

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:07.190
There is an interpreter.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:08.620
So I'm just showing that.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:11.230
But what I'm doing-- what I
really want to do is I'm going

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:14.020
to run this Python function
that's going to let me take

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:15.510
that--

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:17.260
an arbitrary tables
of my Hacker News

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:21.650
sample and pull that
into a Spark data frame.

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.750
Now once that's into
a Spark data frame,

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:25.510
I'm just going to
do a quick count

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:28.720
to establish that, hey, there's
a thousand rows in that sample.

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:31.893
That matches my limit, so I'm
probably on the right track.

00:23:31.893 --> 00:23:33.310
Now what I want
to do is I'm going

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:35.953
to register this data frame.

00:23:35.953 --> 00:23:38.620
And so that-- with one line, I'm
going to register my data frame

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:39.815
as a temp table.

00:23:39.815 --> 00:23:42.190
And that's going to let me
work with this same exact data

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:45.760
frame across all the
languages of Spark.

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:48.280
So that's Scala,
SQL, Python, R. I

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:51.370
can go back and forth without
having to move infrastructure

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:52.060
or data around.

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:53.750
It's all in the same data frame.

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:55.480
And you can see,
now, that I can start

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:58.840
to do some of my
basic R explorations,

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:03.820
things that look completely
normal, like filtering on data

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.460
for scores greater than 50.

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:08.710
But I can now do this
on cloud-scale data sets

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:13.852
instead of just the R
that's running on my laptop.

00:24:13.852 --> 00:24:15.310
Now there's two
ways I can actually

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.190
scale analysis using Spark.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.740
First off, this is an
example where I'm just

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.680
going to tune a bunch--

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:27.183
send a GLM across
Spark and send it

00:24:27.183 --> 00:24:28.850
with a lot of different
hyperparameters.

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:31.000
So I can tune a lot of
smaller models at once

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:34.060
with different hyperparameters,
get those all on a list,

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.580
and then analyze which model
maybe performed the best.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.580
But in this example here,
I'm actually not going

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:40.750
to do that list apply.

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:42.070
I'm going to do a de-apply.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:45.730
Because I just want
to run one NLP package

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:50.590
that I love in Spark across that
whole data set of Hacker News.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:53.170
Now in this case, I just
have my thousand samples

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.042
that came back.

00:24:54.042 --> 00:24:55.750
But this is enough
where I'm saying, yep,

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:57.770
I think I'm on the right
track with this model.

00:24:57.770 --> 00:24:59.890
So what I want to
do now is I'm going

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:02.380
to go back up to
the function where

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:05.447
I was calling my sample table.

00:25:05.447 --> 00:25:07.780
And I'm now going to change
that to the full Hacker News

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:12.460
data set-- so the entire corpus
of notes, not just my sample.

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.270
And then just by
clicking this off,

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.480
I'm going to rerun all
that same code you just

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:20.290
saw, except this time, on the
full Hacker News data set.

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:21.970
And as this kicks
off, what I'd real--

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:23.470
as this kicks off,
what I want to do

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:25.930
is come back into Dataproc
and take a look at what's

00:25:25.930 --> 00:25:27.790
happening underneath the hood.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:30.100
So I started with
three VMs, which

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:33.090
is totally fine for
my thousand-node

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:35.530
as I was manipulating the
data and getting ready.

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:38.260
But about two minutes later,
on the larger data set,

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:40.660
as I'm pulling that
data in from BigQuery,

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.300
the Dataproc autoscaler
says, hey, you

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.467
need a little bit more help.

00:25:43.467 --> 00:25:45.940
Let me add a couple
nodes to your cluster.

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:47.980
Then, a few minutes
later, there's

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:50.990
another spike in the
yarn memory usage,

00:25:50.990 --> 00:25:55.830
which is because I'm actually
now training on that text data.

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:58.860
So the cluster got even bigger.

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:00.570
But once that model
training completes,

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.070
I just refresh the page.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:04.320
And the Dataproc autoscaler
has said, OK, I've

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:05.610
used all the memory I need.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.010
And it scales my cluster
back down for me.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.350
And so all of that was able
to occur without me ever

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:11.800
changing from the notebook.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:13.080
I didn't change environments.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:16.440
I just clicked Run, and
Cloud Dataprco autoscaler

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:18.250
knew how to respond.

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:20.812
Finally, if I wanted to
save this model off and not

00:26:20.812 --> 00:26:22.770
keep a running cluster,
but have something else

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:26.040
around-- have it around, I could
create a workflow template.

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:28.600
And I'm not going to step
through all of the steps here.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:30.630
But essentially, it
would end up looking

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:33.900
like this in the console,
where, at any time, if I wanted

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:36.450
to rerun all this with
the associated cluster,

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:37.495
I would just click Run.

00:26:37.495 --> 00:26:38.370
And that would be it.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:45.370
All right, we can flip
back to the slide deck now.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.700
So if you want to go,
and get started, and do

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:49.950
that exact same
analysis, this code

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:51.820
is what I used to
spin up the cluster.

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:53.130
It's pretty straightforward.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.130
It's just I'm calling
into Cloud Dataproc.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.080
I'm saying, use the
optional components

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.930
of Jupyter, Zeppelin, Anaconda.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:03.930
In this example, you could
also throw in RStudio.

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:07.120
I used the Zeppelin, because
I want to go cross-languages.

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:08.670
But you could
definitely use RStudio

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:10.740
with this initialization action.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:12.930
And then I installed
a BigQuery connector

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:15.480
and the enable
component gateway.

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:17.970
That's what exposed
those web UI links for me

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.320
and made sure that I had
permission to get into them.

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:22.170
And finally, the
auto-scaling policy--

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:25.680
you can actually tune the
auto-scaling policy to--

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:27.660
there's a lot of
knobs that we expose.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.275
If you don't care
about those knobs,

00:27:29.275 --> 00:27:30.400
you don't have to use them.

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:32.573
But they are exposed,
if you want to,

00:27:32.573 --> 00:27:33.990
in the settings
of the autoscaler,

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:38.110
to how aggressive you want it
or how often you want to scale.

00:27:38.110 --> 00:27:40.560
So with that, I'm going
to hand over to Greg.

00:27:49.182 --> 00:27:50.140
GREG MIKELS: All right.

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:52.057
Thanks, everyone, for
joining us this morning.

00:27:52.057 --> 00:27:54.520
So we heard about some great
ways to leverage TensorFlow

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:56.562
and Spark ecosystems
using R and GCP.

00:27:56.562 --> 00:27:59.020
But what about the cases where
you need to train and deploy

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.000
a model using any R library?

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:05.250
So let's look at an
end-to-end solution for this.

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:08.390
So first we need to set up
an R development environment.

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:10.520
And Cloud ML Notebooks
is a great tool

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:13.050
on Google Cloud for data
scientists to do this.

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:14.900
We can get started
with one click,

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:17.710
easily scale up with Compute
Engine machine types,

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:20.930
add or remove GPUs as
necessary, leverage both R

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:23.990
and Python from the notebook,
and enable team collaboration

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:27.470
with Git integration
and Cloud AI Hub.

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:30.800
We can publish notebooks
to Cloud AI Hub.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.950
Specifically, for R users, we
can get started in four steps.

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:35.840
So we can launch a
PyTorch ML Notebook

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:36.962
which has Conda installed.

00:28:36.962 --> 00:28:38.420
And this is going
to make it easier

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:42.320
to install our R-based
package and libraries.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.020
We're also going to
load the rpy2 extension

00:28:45.020 --> 00:28:46.670
to enable R cell magic.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:48.350
And this is what
allows us to run both R

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.810
and Python in the same notebook.

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:53.480
We're going to create a
directory in our home folder

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:54.270
for libraries.

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.600
So we can install
our own libraries

00:28:56.600 --> 00:29:01.082
and have write access
to that directory.

00:29:01.082 --> 00:29:02.790
But what should we do
in those situations

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:04.560
where resources on our
development workstation

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:06.560
aren't adequate, like
running out of memory when

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.580
working with a large data set?

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:11.370
So let's look at how we can
package R code in containers

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:14.632
for running on Cloud ML
Engine and Kubernetes Engine.

00:29:14.632 --> 00:29:16.590
For those just getting
started with containers,

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:18.548
you can think of them as
a method for packaging

00:29:18.548 --> 00:29:21.360
an app with its
dependencies for running

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:24.120
on a variety of platforms,
not just on ML Engine.

00:29:26.823 --> 00:29:29.240
So in the next demo, I'll walk
through building a pipeline

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:30.860
based on this architecture.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.560
We're going to import and
analyze our data with BigQuery.

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:35.360
So we're going to
use standard SQL

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:39.770
to query our data set, stage it
as a CSV file in Google Cloud

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:41.150
Storage.

00:29:41.150 --> 00:29:43.430
We'll write a training
application with Jupyter,

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:45.560
with ML Notebooks
and R. And we'll

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:49.490
package this up with Docker
and send it over to ML Engine.

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:51.797
We'll write the app such
that the assets, the model

00:29:51.797 --> 00:29:53.630
assets that are created
by the training app,

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.207
are stored in Google
Cloud Storage.

00:29:56.207 --> 00:29:58.040
And we'll take these
assets and package them

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:01.020
in another container that
we can deploy for serving.

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:04.330
And this will leverage Google
Kubernetes Engine, seldon,

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:04.830
and s2i.

00:30:07.465 --> 00:30:08.926
All right-- demo.

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:15.440
All right, great,
so first, we'll

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:20.360
navigate over to ML
Engine and ML Notebooks.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:23.403
I already have-- well, we could
create a new instance, PyTorch.

00:30:23.403 --> 00:30:25.070
But I already have
one created, so we're

00:30:25.070 --> 00:30:26.403
going to open this one to start.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:30.140
And I'm going to--

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:31.868
I pulled some code from GitHub.

00:30:31.868 --> 00:30:34.160
So I'm going to start here
with a notebook I've already

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:36.060
created.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:36.870
I have Conda.

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:38.730
I've used Candida to
install rpy2 already,

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:41.510
so I'll go ahead and
load the extension.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:44.313
I've created a directory
for my libraries.

00:30:44.313 --> 00:30:45.980
And I can go and check
this out, and see

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.355
that I've installed
some already,

00:30:47.355 --> 00:30:49.590
and I have access to them.

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:51.900
And then I can use
the R cell magic next

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:55.208
to install whatever
library I need.

00:30:55.208 --> 00:30:57.000
And then we want to
get data from BigQuery.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:59.160
So we can use the
BigQuery cell magic.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:00.840
And then our data
will be exported

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:03.867
into a data frame, which
we can then import into R.

00:31:03.867 --> 00:31:06.450
So then, once it's in R, we can
view the structure of the data

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.380
that we've pulled in.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:12.430
Here we're looking at public
baby weight data from BigQuery.

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:13.810
And we can also build a model--

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:17.020
just a traditional linear
model-- just to get started.

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.430
But we want to do
more than that.

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:20.260
We want it-- so
let's take this data

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:22.360
and stage it in Cloud
Storage so that we

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.880
can run some other models,
and we can scale it up

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:26.590
to ML Engine.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:30.060
So I'm going to go into
the BigQuery console,

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:33.470
and take that query,
and paste it in there.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:36.700
And this can be done from
within R using the API,

00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:39.490
but it's just as easy
to use the console.

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:45.350
So first, I'm querying about
68,000 rows, just for testing.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:47.650
So I'm going to
have two CSV files.

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:52.010
And I'll export this directly
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:53.900
And this is my storage bucket.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:58.160
The train data's small,
is going to be that file.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.330
And then I'll change this query
to sample a bit more data.

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:04.733
And we'll use this data set
for when we scale to ML Engine.

00:32:04.733 --> 00:32:06.400
So when this finishes,
we'll see that we

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:08.940
have about 13 million rows.

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:10.860
You can also see that
we're doing a little bit

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:12.610
of pre-processing in the query.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:14.550
And it's good to keep
in mind that BigQuery

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:17.670
is very performant for
pre-processing data with SQL.

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:21.120
So this would be a
good case to use SQL

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:24.450
for some of that instead
of R, potentially.

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:27.180
So we'll export this
CSV to Cloud Storage.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:32.330
And now we've got my data--

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.820
I've got my data
staged for training.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:36.560
So I'll head back
over to Jupyter,

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:40.320
and I'll start working on
writing my training app.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.720
So first, I create
this trainer folder

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:45.147
that you can see on
the left sidebar there.

00:32:45.147 --> 00:32:47.230
And I'm going to create a
file for installing my R

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:48.920
dependencies.

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:51.200
First, I'm going to set
a library path based

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:52.910
on an environment variable.

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:55.675
And then I'll specify
the packages I need.

00:32:55.675 --> 00:32:57.050
And then in the
training app, I'm

00:32:57.050 --> 00:33:00.830
going to specify that
same library path.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.100
I'm going to configure command
line arguments for Google Cloud

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:06.940
Storage for training data
for the export directory.

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:09.740
I'm going to use caret to
impute missing values, center

00:33:09.740 --> 00:33:11.700
and scale my data.

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:13.670
I'm going to change
the factors--

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:15.950
or the features I
need to factors,

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:18.380
partition the data
into test and train,

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:21.950
set up hyperparameter
tuning, cross-validation.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:24.290
And then I can kick
off my training job

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:27.145
using glmnet for this example.

00:33:27.145 --> 00:33:28.520
And when the
training's complete,

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:30.710
it's going to save my
model file, as well

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:33.890
as my pre-processing weights,
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:33:33.890 --> 00:33:35.962
And I could also export
any additional assets

00:33:35.962 --> 00:33:37.670
that I wanted, like
if I wanted to export

00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:40.700
plots or other results.

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:43.400
Then I'll go into Docker
to package all of this.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:44.720
I'll take the base R image.

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:46.820
I'll install the Google
SDK so I can download

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:49.040
my files from Cloud Storage.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:51.260
I'll copy over that file
to-- or that script--

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:55.870
to install packages, set the
library environment variable.

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:58.000
I'll copy over the training app.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.407
And then I'll use entry
point to tell ML Engine, hey,

00:34:00.407 --> 00:34:01.990
this is the script
that's going to run

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:05.160
when the container kicks off.

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:08.710
So before we go ahead and deploy
the container to Container

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:12.380
Registry, let's just make sure
the training app runs locally.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:14.112
And then we'll get
the model file saved.

00:34:14.112 --> 00:34:15.570
And then we'll also
test inference.

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.090
This is an end-to-end
deployment,

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:19.219
so we want to make sure
inference works, as well

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:21.409
as training, before we go
ahead and start training

00:34:21.409 --> 00:34:22.699
on ML Engine.

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:24.167
So we see we get
a result returned.

00:34:24.167 --> 00:34:26.000
So we'll go ahead and
build the Docker image

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.469
and package up our
training container.

00:34:29.469 --> 00:34:32.530
And then we can deploy it to
Google Container Registry.

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:36.940
And once it's in
Container Registry,

00:34:36.940 --> 00:34:38.320
it's accessible to ML Engine.

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:41.139
So we'll submit a job
referencing that container,

00:34:41.139 --> 00:34:43.989
reference our training data in
the storage bucket, the output

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:45.340
directory.

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:47.170
And we can also
specify a machine type

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:51.833
or a cluster of machines here
with accelerators if necessary.

00:34:51.833 --> 00:34:54.500
So when the job is submitted, we
can go back over to the console

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:56.360
and use ML Engine to
view the job running.

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:00.940
We'll be able to
see our utilization

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:04.030
metrics and our logs.

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:09.155
And you can add
additional logs as needed

00:35:09.155 --> 00:35:10.280
based on your training app.

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:16.250
When the model is
complete, the model assets

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:17.730
are written to Cloud Storage.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:21.110
So we can go into Cloud
Storage and see the assets

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:22.760
from the prior
jobs that have run.

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:24.740
So I've got a few folders
here from prior jobs.

00:35:24.740 --> 00:35:27.530
And we'll look at
the most recent.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:29.510
And here we can see
the model RDS file

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:33.330
and the pre-processing weights.

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:34.870
So then let's go
back into Jupyter.

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:38.120
And let's serve our
completed model.

00:35:38.120 --> 00:35:40.840
So we're going to install
s2i, which is source to image.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:42.798
It's a tool that's just
going to make it easier

00:35:42.798 --> 00:35:45.220
to build the container
image without a Docker file

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:46.210
essentially.

00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:49.540
Source to image and seldon
require this environment file.

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:51.970
It requires and
install.r file, which

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:53.393
installs the dependencies.

00:35:53.393 --> 00:35:55.810
We're going to download the
model assets from Google Cloud

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:58.111
Storage and include
them in the container.

00:36:00.915 --> 00:36:03.040
And then we're going to
setup a runtime file, which

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:06.340
is going to load the RDS model
file, the pre-processing file.

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:08.530
It's going to take the
input from prediction,

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:11.920
do the pre-processing, and then
evaluate against the model.

00:36:14.512 --> 00:36:15.970
So once we have
all these files, we

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.405
can call s2i to build
our serving image.

00:36:18.405 --> 00:36:19.780
So very easy-- we
don't even have

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:21.250
to build a Docker file here.

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.630
We just build our
R files for serving

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:25.480
and then package up our image.

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:29.700
We can test it locally and
then run a request against it

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.370
locally.

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:33.670
We see we get what
we were expecting.

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:37.350
So we can also view logs if we
needed to debug, and then stop

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:39.497
the container to restart it.

00:36:39.497 --> 00:36:41.830
So we'll go ahead and push
the working container that we

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:43.570
have to Container Registry.

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:51.075
And if we didn't have a
cluster already started,

00:36:51.075 --> 00:36:53.660
we would create one,
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:55.660
And we can see that we
already have one running.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:58.830
So we'll deploy the application
to the cluster we have running,

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:00.480
and then verify that
it's been deployed

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:04.510
and that the pod is running
for the application.

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:07.990
And then we'll expose the
deployment on port 80,

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:11.932
and then get an external
IP address for the app.

00:37:11.932 --> 00:37:13.390
So we have our
external IP address.

00:37:13.390 --> 00:37:14.890
We can make another request.

00:37:14.890 --> 00:37:16.690
And we see the result
we were expecting.

00:37:16.690 --> 00:37:19.590
So it looks like
we're good to go.

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:22.470
A nice thing with Kubernetes
Engine, or managed Kubernetes

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:24.490
service, we can go
into the console

00:37:24.490 --> 00:37:27.400
and view the pod, the
application running.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.050
We can see utilization metrics.

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:33.482
And we can view logs from
the container as well.

00:37:33.482 --> 00:37:35.440
So with that, I'll pass
it back over to Mikhail

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:36.940
to wrap up for us.

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:37.440
Thank you.

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:42.610
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Thanks, Greg.

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:45.650
So just in closing,
hopefully this image

00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:47.070
makes a little bit more sense.

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:49.550
I think-- at the very top,
starting with the notebooks,

00:37:49.550 --> 00:37:53.060
I think some of the key ways
we saw Chris talk about the new

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:55.940
web interfaces module within
Dataproc that allows you

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.740
to quickly spin up
these notebooks--

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:01.880
ML Notebooks actually is
a managed Jupyter service

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:05.390
that went, also, beta on March
1-- so also a new service

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:08.360
that maybe a lot of you may
not have heard of before.

00:38:08.360 --> 00:38:11.090
But we really made sure
that both demos as well as

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:13.860
my reference architecture
showcased all three

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.640
so you got a sense of how
each of those three look like.

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:18.950
We also walked through
different storage techniques,

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:22.010
both from BigQuery
as well as GCS.

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:23.810
And from a Spark
perspective, I think

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:26.150
the auto-scaling is
something that I've really

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:28.340
been using quite a bit
myself as well as working

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:29.340
with a lot of customers.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:31.730
Because there is no need to
then necessarily right-size

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:34.580
your workloads, or maybe have
a little bit more capacity

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:36.130
than you need sitting idle.

00:38:36.130 --> 00:38:38.960
That auto-scaling feature
really has a certain logic

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:41.683
built in when the
number of clusters grow.

00:38:41.683 --> 00:38:43.100
And then on the
very bottom right,

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:45.440
what Greg walked
through, we'll make

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:48.560
sure to provide that in
terms of GitHub code.

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:50.930
But the key takeaway is,
again, hybrid and portable.

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:52.460
These are all containers.

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:53.960
You can use them
on Google Cloud.

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:56.060
You can train or create
a serving application.

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:58.280
But those are fully
portable to be ported over

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:00.770
to another cloud,
onto on-premise.

00:39:00.770 --> 00:39:02.983
And seldon specifically,
the serving

00:39:02.983 --> 00:39:04.400
approach that Greg
walked through,

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:07.160
that's part of the broader
Kubeflow ecosystem, where

00:39:07.160 --> 00:39:09.605
we are making it very easy--

00:39:09.605 --> 00:39:13.160
easy connectors to the
Kubernetes ecosystem.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:17.180
A quick cheat sheet around both
the services and libraries--

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:18.150
it is quite a bit.

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:20.450
And we'll try to get some
resources out there online.

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:22.070
But the first two
rows really talk

00:39:22.070 --> 00:39:24.980
about the data
ingestion, GCP services,

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:28.520
as well as the R packages that
allow you to access these very

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:29.860
quickly, directly in our code.

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:32.360
We walked through different
ways of doing that within your R

00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:34.670
code as well as
directly in the console,

00:39:34.670 --> 00:39:37.320
whatever you're more comfortable
with-- various options there.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:39.737
And then the bottom three talk
about the training service,

00:39:39.737 --> 00:39:42.320
the serving service, and
then the actual R library

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:44.930
packages that you can
leverage to access

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:47.060
that in a familiar environment.

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:49.157
Now the last column,
of course, it

00:39:49.157 --> 00:39:51.240
does require a little bit
of container knowledge--

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:55.130
Docker, Kubernetes-- but really
does future-proof yourself,

00:39:55.130 --> 00:39:57.200
and makes a lot of
your R workloads

00:39:57.200 --> 00:39:58.890
portable moving forward.

00:39:58.890 --> 00:39:59.840
So what now?

